The critical pattern is streaming — never load the entire file into memory. `BlobClient.upload()` accepts a `BinaryData` built from an `InputStream`, and `BlobClient.openInputStream()` returns a stream you can pipe directly to the HTTP response.

```java
@Service
public class BlobStorageService {

    private final BlobContainerClient container;

    public BlobStorageService(BlobServiceClient client) {
        this.container = client.getBlobContainerClient("uploads");
    }

    public void upload(String name, InputStream data, long length) {
        BlobClient blob = container.getBlobClient(name);
        blob.upload(BinaryData.fromStream(data, length), true);
    }

    public InputStream download(String name) {
        return container.getBlobClient(name).openInputStream();
    }
}
```

The `true` parameter on `upload()` enables overwrite — without it, uploading to an existing blob name throws an exception. For files larger than 256 MB, the SDK automatically splits the upload into 4 MB blocks and uploads them in parallel. You can tune this with `ParallelTransferOptions`: set `setBlockSizeLong(8 * 1024 * 1024)` for 8 MB blocks and `setMaxConcurrency(4)` for four parallel upload streams.

In the REST controller, pipe `MultipartFile.getInputStream()` directly to the blob upload — never call `getBytes()`. This keeps memory usage constant regardless of file size.

Proxying every download through your Spring Boot server wastes bandwidth and server threads. A better pattern is generating a **Shared Access Signature (SAS)** URL with read-only permissions and a short expiration, then redirecting the client to fetch the blob directly from Azure.

```java
public String generateDownloadUrl(String blobName, Duration validFor) {
    BlobClient blob = container.getBlobClient(blobName);
    BlobSasPermission perms = new BlobSasPermission()
        .setReadPermission(true);
    BlobServiceSasSignatureValues sasValues =
        new BlobServiceSasSignatureValues(
            OffsetDateTime.now().plus(validFor), perms);
    return blob.getBlobUrl() + "?" + blob.generateSas(sasValues);
}
```

The SAS URL is scoped to a single blob with read-only access, and it expires after the duration you specify — typically 15 minutes to one hour. The client downloads directly from Azure's CDN-backed storage infrastructure, which is faster and cheaper than routing through your server.

For upload scenarios, you can generate SAS tokens with write permissions and let clients upload directly to Blob Storage, bypassing your server entirely. This is common for large file uploads in web applications — the frontend requests a write SAS URL from your API, uploads directly to Azure, and notifies your API when the upload completes. This pattern eliminates the file-too-large-for-server problem and scales downloads independently of your API servers.

Azure AD's authorization model separates authentication from authorization through two mechanisms: **app roles** and **group memberships**. App roles are defined in your application's manifest in the Azure portal and are explicitly assigned to users, groups, or service principals. When a user authenticates, Azure AD includes their assigned roles in the JWT's `roles` claim.

Spring Cloud Azure maps these roles to Spring Security authorities with the prefix `APPROLE_`. An app role named `Admin` becomes authority `APPROLE_Admin`, and you use it in your security configuration.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()));
        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasAuthority("APPROLE_Admin")
            .requestMatchers("/api/reports/**").hasAuthority("APPROLE_Analyst")
            .anyRequest().authenticated()
        );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

App roles are preferred over groups for application-level authorization because they're scoped to your application — a group membership like 'Engineering' is a tenant-wide concept that doesn't map cleanly to API permissions. Groups are better suited for broad access policies, while app roles model fine-grained permissions specific to your application's domain.

Cold start is the primary technical challenge for Java Functions. The mitigation strategies span from infrastructure choices to code architecture.

The **Premium plan** keeps a minimum number of instances pre-warmed. Unlike the Consumption plan, instances don't scale to zero — at least one JVM is always running. This eliminates cold start for the first request but costs roughly $150/month for the smallest tier.

```bash
az functionapp plan create \
  --name my-premium-plan \
  --resource-group my-rg \
  --sku EP1 \
  --min-instances 1
```

For functions that must run on the Consumption plan, keep the function lean. Avoid bootstrapping a full Spring context — use plain Java with manual dependency construction. A function that creates its own service objects in the constructor starts in 2–3 seconds versus 10–15 seconds with the Spring Cloud Function adapter.

```java
@FunctionName("processOrder")
public HttpResponseMessage run(
    @HttpTrigger(name = "req", methods = {HttpMethod.POST},
        route = "orders") HttpRequestMessage<String> request,
    ExecutionContext context) {
    OrderRequest order = objectMapper.readValue(
        request.getBody(), OrderRequest.class);
    OrderResult result = orderService.process(order);
    return request.createResponseBuilder(HttpStatus.OK)
        .body(result).build();
}
```

For timer-triggered and queue-triggered functions, cold start is rarely a concern — a 3-second delay on a nightly cleanup job is invisible. Cold start matters primarily for HTTP-triggered functions with latency SLOs.

The `DefaultAzureCredential` class tries multiple authentication sources in a fixed order, stopping at the first one that succeeds. Understanding this chain explains why the same code works across environments without configuration changes.

```
1. EnvironmentCredential      → Reads AZURE_CLIENT_ID, TENANT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET
2. WorkloadIdentityCredential  → AKS pod with federated service account
3. ManagedIdentityCredential   → App Service, Functions, VM metadata endpoint
4. AzureCliCredential          → Local development via az login
5. AzurePowerShellCredential   → Local via PowerShell login
6. IntelliJCredential          → IntelliJ Azure plugin
```

In production on App Service or Functions, step 3 succeeds — the Managed Identity credential acquires a token from `http://169.254.169.254/metadata/identity/oauth2/token`, an endpoint only accessible from within the Azure instance. On a developer's laptop, steps 1–3 fail silently, and step 4 picks up the developer's `az login` session. In a CI/CD pipeline, environment variables are set, and step 1 succeeds.

The key insight is that `DefaultAzureCredential` makes environment-specific authentication invisible to your application code. You wire a single `TokenCredential` bean and inject it wherever Azure SDK clients are created.

```java
@Bean
public TokenCredential credential() {
    return new DefaultAzureCredentialBuilder().build();
}

@Bean
public SecretClient keyVault(TokenCredential cred) {
    return new SecretClientBuilder()
        .vaultUrl("https://my-vault.vault.azure.net")
        .credential(cred)
        .buildClient();
}
```

One subtlety: for **user-assigned Managed Identities**, you must specify the client ID in the builder — `new DefaultAzureCredentialBuilder().managedIdentityClientId("<id>").build()`. Without this, `DefaultAzureCredential` uses the system-assigned identity, and if the resource has both types assigned, it may pick the wrong one.

Publishing is straightforward — `PubSubTemplate.publish()` serializes your object to JSON, sends it to a topic, and returns a future you can use for async confirmation or error handling. The critical detail is that publishing is **fire-and-forget by default** — if you don't attach a callback or block on the future, a publish failure is silently swallowed.

The setup requires one starter dependency and minimal configuration. Spring Cloud GCP detects Application Default Credentials automatically, so no credential configuration is needed when running on Cloud Run or GKE.

```java
@Service
public class OrderEventPublisher {
    private final PubSubTemplate pubSubTemplate;

    public OrderEventPublisher(PubSubTemplate pubSubTemplate) {
        this.pubSubTemplate = pubSubTemplate;
    }

    public void publishOrderCreated(OrderEvent event) {
        pubSubTemplate.publish("order-events", event)
            .whenComplete((msgId, ex) -> {
                if (ex != null) {
                    log.error("Publish failed for order {}", event.orderId(), ex);
                } else {
                    log.info("Published order {} as {}", event.orderId(), msgId);
                }
            });
    }
}
```

The `whenComplete` callback is essential for production — without it, a network partition or topic misconfiguration would silently drop events. For critical events like payments, block on the future with `.get(5, SECONDS)` to guarantee delivery before returning a response to the caller.

Pub/Sub uses **at-least-once delivery**: every message is delivered at least once, and it keeps redelivering until the consumer explicitly acknowledges it. If your consumer throws an exception before calling `ack()`, the message goes back to the subscription and is redelivered — potentially infinitely if the bug is deterministic.

This is the most common Pub/Sub production bug. A consumer processes an order, updates the database, but crashes before the ack. The message redelivers, the order is processed again, and the customer gets charged twice. The fix requires both **manual acknowledgement** and **idempotent processing**.

Spring Cloud GCP's channel adapter defaults to `AckMode.AUTO`, which acks on successful handler return. For production, switch to `AckMode.MANUAL` so you control exactly when acknowledgement happens.

```java
@Configuration
public class PubSubConsumerConfig {
    @Bean
    public PubSubInboundChannelAdapter orderAdapter(
            @Qualifier("orderChannel") MessageChannel channel,
            PubSubTemplate template) {
        var adapter = new PubSubInboundChannelAdapter(template, "order-events-sub");
        adapter.setOutputChannel(channel);
        adapter.setAckMode(AckMode.MANUAL); // explicit control
        return adapter;
    }
}

@Component
public class OrderConsumer {
    @ServiceActivator(inputChannel = "orderChannel")
    public void handle(Message<OrderEvent> msg) {
        var ack = msg.getHeaders().get(
            GcpPubSubHeaders.ORIGINAL_MESSAGE,
            BasicAcknowledgeablePubsubMessage.class);
        try {
            processOrder(msg.getPayload()); // idempotent operation
            ack.ack();  // only after successful processing
        } catch (TransientException e) {
            ack.nack(); // redelivery for retryable errors
        } catch (PoisonMessageException e) {
            log.error("Poison message: {}", msg.getPayload());
            ack.ack();  // ack poison messages to stop infinite redelivery
        }
    }
}
```

Notice the deliberate choice with poison messages: we **ack them** to stop redelivery, but log the error. The better solution is a dead-letter topic that captures these automatically.

Property source resolution handles startup-time secrets, but some scenarios require fetching secrets at runtime: **rotating encryption keys** without restarting the application, **per-tenant API keys** where the secret name is dynamic, or **secret creation** during automated onboarding flows.

`SecretManagerTemplate` provides programmatic access for these cases. Unlike the property source, it can fetch specific versions, create new secrets, and add versions — all at runtime.

```java
@Service
public class EncryptionKeyService {
    private final SecretManagerTemplate secretManager;

    public EncryptionKeyService(SecretManagerTemplate secretManager) {
        this.secretManager = secretManager;
    }

    public String getCurrentKey(String tenantId) {
        // Dynamic secret name based on tenant
        return secretManager.getSecretString(
            "projects/my-project/secrets/encrypt-key-" + tenantId + "/versions/latest");
    }

    public void rotateKey(String tenantId, byte[] newKey) {
        String secretId = "encrypt-key-" + tenantId;
        secretManager.createSecretIfMissing(secretId);
        secretManager.addSecretVersion(secretId, newKey);
    }
}
```

For automated rotation, the pattern is: rotate the secret in Secret Manager, then trigger a redeployment (on Cloud Run, deploy a new revision pointing to the new version) or a Spring Cloud Config refresh (which re-resolves the property source). A Pub/Sub notification on secret version creation can automate this trigger, giving you fully automated credential rotation without manual intervention or plaintext exposure at any point in the chain.

The Java client uses parameterized queries — not string concatenation — for both security (SQL injection prevention) and cost control (query plan caching). The `QueryJobConfiguration` builder lets you set parameters, disable legacy SQL, and critically, set `maximumBytesBilled` to cap the data scanned.

Setting `maximumBytesBilled` is the most important production configuration. Without it, a mistyped WHERE clause or a missing partition filter can scan terabytes and generate a surprise bill. With it, BigQuery rejects the query before scanning if the estimate exceeds your cap.

```java
@Service
public class AnalyticsService {
    private final BigQuery bigQuery = BigQueryOptions.getDefaultInstance().getService();

    public List<SalesReport> getDailySales(String date) throws InterruptedException {
        String query = """
            SELECT product_id, SUM(quantity) AS units, SUM(revenue) AS total
            FROM `my-project.sales.transactions`
            WHERE DATE(order_ts) = @report_date AND status = 'COMPLETED'
            GROUP BY product_id
            ORDER BY total DESC LIMIT 100
            """;

        QueryJobConfiguration config = QueryJobConfiguration.newBuilder(query)
            .addNamedParameter("report_date", QueryParameterValue.date(date))
            .setUseLegacySql(false)
            .setMaximumBytesBilled(10L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024) // 10 GB cap
            .build();

        TableResult result = bigQuery.query(config);
        return StreamSupport.stream(result.iterateAll().spliterator(), false)
            .map(row -> new SalesReport(
                row.get("product_id").getStringValue(),
                row.get("units").getLongValue(),
                row.get("total").getDoubleValue()))
            .toList();
    }
}
```

The `.query()` call blocks until results are ready — typically 2-30 seconds depending on data volume. For queries that might run longer, use `bigQuery.create(JobInfo.of(config))` to create an async job, then poll with `job.waitFor()` or check status via the job ID.

The `storage.writer()` method returns a `WriteChannel` that streams bytes to Cloud Storage in chunks. The JVM only ever holds one buffer's worth of data — typically 8 KB or 64 KB — regardless of whether the file is 1 KB or 10 GB.

This is a **resumable upload** under the hood: if the connection drops mid-transfer, the client library retries from the last successfully written chunk, not from the beginning. For large files over unreliable networks, this means the difference between a successful upload and an infinite retry loop.

```java
@Service
public class FileStorageService {
    private final Storage storage = StorageOptions.getDefaultInstance().getService();
    private static final String BUCKET = "my-app-uploads";

    public String upload(String name, InputStream content, String contentType)
            throws IOException {
        BlobInfo info = BlobInfo.newBuilder(BlobId.of(BUCKET, name))
            .setContentType(contentType)
            .build();
        try (WriteChannel writer = storage.writer(info)) {
            byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
            int n;
            while ((n = content.read(buf)) != -1) {
                writer.write(ByteBuffer.wrap(buf, 0, n));
            }
        }
        return String.format("gs://%s/%s", BUCKET, name);
    }
}
```

The `try-with-resources` on `WriteChannel` is important — closing the channel finalizes the upload. If you forget to close it, the object remains in an incomplete state and isn't visible in the bucket.

The highest-impact pattern for file-heavy applications is **signed URLs**. Instead of proxying a 200 MB file download through your Spring Boot server — consuming network bandwidth, heap memory, and a Tomcat thread for the entire transfer duration — you generate a time-limited URL and redirect the client to download directly from Cloud Storage.

The same applies to uploads: a signed PUT URL lets the browser upload directly to Cloud Storage. Your server only handles the URL generation (milliseconds), not the data transfer (seconds to minutes).

```java
public URL signedDownloadUrl(String objectName, int expiryMinutes) {
    BlobInfo info = BlobInfo.newBuilder(BlobId.of(BUCKET, objectName)).build();
    return storage.signUrl(info, expiryMinutes, TimeUnit.MINUTES,
        Storage.SignUrlOption.withV4Signature());
}

public URL signedUploadUrl(String objectName, String contentType, int expiryMinutes) {
    BlobInfo info = BlobInfo.newBuilder(BlobId.of(BUCKET, objectName))
        .setContentType(contentType).build();
    return storage.signUrl(info, expiryMinutes, TimeUnit.MINUTES,
        Storage.SignUrlOption.httpMethod(HttpMethod.PUT),
        Storage.SignUrlOption.withV4Signature());
}
```

V4 signatures are the current standard — they're AWS S3-compatible and expire correctly. The expiry time is a security decision: long enough for the transfer to complete, short enough that a leaked URL doesn't provide indefinite access. For downloads, 15-30 minutes is typical. For uploads, match the expected upload duration plus buffer.

On the infrastructure side, enable **Uniform Bucket-Level Access** to enforce IAM-only permissions. This removes per-object ACLs — a common source of accidental public exposure when developers set object-level ACLs without realizing the bucket policy is more restrictive.

GCP uses two types of tokens, and confusing them is a common source of "403 Forbidden" errors in service-to-service calls.

**Access tokens** authorize calls to GCP APIs — Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage, BigQuery. They're scoped to API permissions and are what ADC provides by default. You never manually handle these; the client libraries manage them.

**Identity tokens (OIDC)** prove your service's identity to another HTTP service. When Service A calls Service B on Cloud Run, Service A presents an identity token with Service B's URL as the audience. Service B (if configured with `--no-allow-unauthenticated`) validates the token and confirms the caller is who they claim to be.

The Java implementation uses `IdTokenCredentials` from the `google-auth-library`.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentServiceClient {
    private static final String PAYMENT_URL = "https://payment-svc-abc.run.app";

    public PaymentResult charge(PaymentRequest req) throws IOException {
        IdTokenCredentials creds = IdTokenCredentials.newBuilder()
            .setIdTokenProvider(
                (IdTokenProvider) ServiceAccountCredentials.getApplicationDefault())
            .setTargetAudience(PAYMENT_URL) // audience = the service being called
            .build();
        creds.refresh();

        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.setBearerAuth(creds.getIdToken().getTokenValue());

        return restTemplate.exchange(
            PAYMENT_URL + "/api/charge", HttpMethod.POST,
            new HttpEntity<>(req, headers), PaymentResult.class).getBody();
    }
}
```

The `targetAudience` must match the URL of the service being called — Cloud Run validates this automatically. On the receiving side, Cloud Run handles token verification when deployed with `--no-allow-unauthenticated`, so Service B doesn't need any auth code. For custom verification (e.g., extracting the caller's service account email for authorization logic), use `GoogleIdTokenVerifier` to parse and validate the token in a Spring filter.

Plain-text logs are effectively unsearchable at scale. Cloud Logging with structured JSON logging transforms logs into queryable data — you can filter by severity, service, trace ID, or any custom field you add via MDC.

The Spring Cloud GCP logging starter formats log entries as JSON and includes the trace ID automatically when tracing is enabled. This means every log entry links to its distributed trace in Cloud Trace.

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>com.google.cloud</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-cloud-gcp-starter-logging</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Custom context via MDC (Mapped Diagnostic Context) adds business-relevant fields to every log entry within a request scope. This is the difference between grepping for "error" across millions of lines and querying `jsonPayload.orderId="ORD-12345"` to see every log entry for one specific order across all services.

```java
@Component
public class RequestContextFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req,
            HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        MDC.put("orderId", req.getHeader("X-Order-Id"));
        MDC.put("userId", req.getHeader("X-User-Id"));
        try {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        } finally {
            MDC.clear(); // prevent context leaking to next request
        }
    }
}
```

The `MDC.clear()` in the finally block is critical — without it, thread pool reuse in Tomcat would leak one request's context into the next request's logs, making debugging even more confusing than having no context at all.
